---
import RepoBadge from "../components/RepoBadge";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { REPO_SLUG, REPO_URL, SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";

const loginUrl = "/login";
const signupUrl = "/signup";
---

<Layout
  title={`${SITE_NAME} | Open source sync, still taking shape`}
  description="OpenSync is a work-in-progress open source project for keeping the content you need available across your devices."
>
  <main class="landing-main">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <header class="landing-header">
        <a class="brand-lockup" href="/">
          <span class="brand-lockup__mark">OS</span>
          <span class="brand-lockup__text">{SITE_NAME}</span>
        </a>

        <div class="landing-header__actions">
          <RepoBadge client:load repo={REPO_SLUG} href={REPO_URL} />
          <a class="button button--text" href={loginUrl}>Log in</a>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
        <div class="hero__copy panel panel--soft">
          <p class="section-kicker">Open source sync in progress</p>
          <h1 id="hero-title" class="hero__title">Keep the useful parts of your day within reach.</h1>
          <p class="hero__lede">
            OpenSync starts with clipboard syncing between your own devices, then grows toward a
            broader way to keep files and other content close at hand. The idea is simple on
            purpose: fewer steps, less ceremony, and no cloud-suite heaviness.
          </p>

          <div class="hero__actions">
            <a class="button button--primary" href={loginUrl}>Try the auth flow</a>
            <a class="button button--ghost" href={signupUrl}>Create an account</a>
          </div>

          <div class="hero__meta" aria-label="Project status">
            <span>Now: clipboard sync</span>
            <span>Next: files, shared content, calmer handoffs</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hero__surface panel panel--soft">
          <div class="sync-orbit" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="sync-orbit__ring"></div>
            <div class="sync-orbit__ring sync-orbit__ring--inner"></div>
            <div class="sync-orbit__core">
              <div>
                <strong>OpenSync</strong>
                <span>Your content, ready when you move.</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="sync-chip sync-chip--clipboard">
              Clipboard
              <span>shipping now</span>
            </div>
            <div class="sync-chip sync-chip--files">
              Files
              <span>coming later</span>
            </div>
            <div class="sync-chip sync-chip--share">
              Sharing
              <span>still forming</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="hero__note">
            <p>
              This site is an introduction, not a sales funnel. OpenSync is still WIP, still open,
              and still focused on proving a calmer sync experience before anything else.
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>No pricing sections.</li>
              <li>No inflated product claims.</li>
              <li>Just the shape of the project as it becomes real.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="what-exists-title">
        <div class="landing-section__intro">
          <p class="section-kicker">What exists now</p>
          <h2 id="what-exists-title" class="landing-section__title">A simpler handoff between devices.</h2>
          <p class="landing-section__description">
            The current build is intentionally narrow. It focuses on authenticating once, returning
            to the desktop app cleanly, and proving that sync can feel immediate instead of
            operational.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="landing-stack">
          <article class="landing-item panel panel--elevated">
            <div class="landing-item__index">01</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="landing-item__title">Browser to desktop without friction</h3>
              <p class="landing-item__body">
                The web flow only exists to complete account actions and send you back to the app.
                It should feel like a light bridge, not a separate product.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="landing-item panel panel--elevated">
            <div class="landing-item__index">02</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="landing-item__title">Calm UI, even for technical users</h3>
              <p class="landing-item__body">
                The tone is technically fluent, but the interaction model stays soft. Clear forms,
                crisp buttons, and minimal explanation matter more than feature theater.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="landing-item panel panel--elevated">
            <div class="landing-item__index">03</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="landing-item__title">WIP by design</h3>
              <p class="landing-item__body">
                OpenSync is still finding its final shape. The landing page should say that plainly,
                while still making the project feel serious and worth following.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="landing-section" aria-labelledby="where-next-title">
        <div class="landing-section__intro">
          <p class="section-kicker">Where it can go</p>
          <h2 id="where-next-title" class="landing-section__title">The project is bigger than clipboard sync.</h2>
          <p class="landing-section__description">
            Clipboard sync is the first useful slice because it proves the core idea quickly. The
            broader theme is continuity: files, notes, and other content should feel present
            wherever you pick up work next.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="landing-callout panel panel--soft">
          <div class="landing-callout__grid">
            <div class="landing-callout__cell">
              <strong>Today</strong>
              <p>Authenticated clipboard sync for your own devices, with the web handling light account flow.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="landing-callout__cell">
              <strong>Later</strong>
              <p>More sync surfaces, starting with files and the ability to move useful content with less effort.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="landing-callout__cell">
              <strong>Eventually</strong>
              <p>Hosted infrastructure where it makes sense, without losing the clarity or calm that makes the project interesting.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="landing-callout__cell">
              <strong>Always</strong>
              <p>A simple UI and direct interactions should remain the differentiator, even as the sync surface grows.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="landing-open-source panel panel--soft" aria-labelledby="oss-title">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Open source right now</p>
          <h2 id="oss-title" class="landing-section__title">Follow the work as it becomes a real tool.</h2>
          <p>
            The repo is the best place to see what is shipping, what is still rough, and where the
            next pieces are headed. If the premise resonates, star it, inspect the code, or use the
            auth flow as a preview of the current handoff experience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="landing-header__actions">
          <RepoBadge client:load repo={REPO_SLUG} href={REPO_URL} showName />
          <a class="button button--primary" href={loginUrl}>Open auth flow</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
